      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate fragment carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2006-A-NL1-19.03\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 2368828\n- Page total: 4\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 25 July 2019 → 2019-07-25\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Schneider electric Systems USA, Inc., 38 Neponset Avenue, Foxborough, MA 02035, United States of America\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant (Applicant and Manufacturer)\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\" (electromagnetic water meter) — the schema example categories include \"Gas meter\" etc. Water meter fits.\n- Type designations: 6500W + IMT656W — hmm, \"Type: 6500W + IMT656W\". This could be two models: 6500W and IMT656W. Actually, given the family characteristics, it's likely \"6500W\" sensor and \"IMT656W\" — perhaps the measuring instrument type. Let me record type_designations: [\"6500W\", \"IMT656W\"] since \"+\" suggests combined types. Actually \"Type: 6500W + IMT656W\" — could be one type designation \"6500W + IMT656W\". Given the ambiguity, I'll list both as separate designations.\n- Description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n- Module designation: not applicable — no module info; omit or null. The schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Since not mentioned, omit.\n\n**Model family:** The cert covers a family of instruments (Table 2 \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\"). Models are meter sizes DN25 through DN200 with accuracy class variants. The type designations are 6500W + IMT656W.\n\nThe models in Table 2: DN25, DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200. Each has multiple rows for different accuracy class / ratio variants.\n\n**Model-level matrix from Table 2:**\n\nLet me parse the table carefully.\n\nHeader: Meter size | Accuracy class | Flow rates [m³/h]: Min. Q1, Trans. Q2, Perm. Q3, Over. Q4 | Ratio Q3/Q1\n\nRows:\n- DN25, class 2, row 1: Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400\n- DN25, class 2, row 2: Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400 (rowspan)\n\nWait, DN25 has rowspan=2 for the class \"2\", and two data rows:\n  - 0,025 | 0,04 | 10 | 12,5 (ratio 400 rowspan=2)\n  - 0,04 | 0,064 | 16 | 20\n\nHmm, but two rows with the same accuracy class 2 and same ratio 400 but different flow rates? That's odd but it's what the table says. Possibly different Q3 sizes within DN25 (Q3=10 and Q3=16). Yes — in water meters, a given DN size can have different Q3 values. So DN25 with Q3=10 (ratio 400 → Q1=0.025) and DN25 with Q3=16 (Q1=0.04).\n\nActually wait: Q3/Q1 = 400: for Q3=10, Q1=10/400=0.025 ✓. For Q3=16, Q1=16/400=0.04 ✓. So both are class 2, ratio 400.\n\n- DN40, class 2, two rows:\n  - 0.0625 | 0.1 | 25 | 31.3, ratio 400\n  - 0.1 | 0.16 | 40 | 50, ratio 400\n  Check: 25/400 = 0.0625 ✓; 40/400 = 0.1 ✓.\n\n- DN50, class 2, two rows:\n  - 0.1 | 0.16 | 40 | 50, ratio 400\n  - 0.1575 | 0.252 | 63 | 78.75, ratio 400\n  Check: 40/400=0.1 ✓; 63/400=0.1575 ✓.\n\n- DN65, class 2, two rows (ratio 400):\n  - 0.1575 | 0.25 | 63 | 78.75\n  - 0.25 | 0.4 | 100 | 125\n  Then class 1, one row: 0.4 | 0.64 | 100 | 125, ratio 250\n  Check: 100/400=0.25 ✓; 100/250=0.4 ✓.\n\n- DN80, class 2, two rows (ratio 400):\n  - 0.25 | 0.4 | 100 | 125\n  - 0.4 | 0.64 | 160 | 200\n  Then class 1, two rows:\n  - 0.625 | 1 | 100 | 125, ratio 160\n  - 0.64 | 1.02 | 160 | 200, ratio 250\n  Check: 100/160=0.625 ✓; 160/250=0.64 ✓.\n\n- DN100, class 2, two rows (ratio 400):\n  - 0.4 | 0.64 | 160 | 200\n  - 0.625 | 1 | 250 | 312.5\n  Then class 1, two rows:\n  - 1 | 1.6 | 160 | 200, ratio 160\n  - 1.6 | 250 | 312.5 | 250 → wait the row is \"1 | 1 | 1,6 | 250 | 312,5 | 250\": cells are Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250.\n  \n  Let me recount the DN100 rows. The markdown:\n  ```\n  <tr><td rowspan=\"4\"><strong>DN100</strong></td><td rowspan=\"2\">2</td><td>0,4</td><td>0,64</td><td>160</td><td>200</td><td rowspan=\"2\">400</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>0,625</td><td>1</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>160</td><td>200</td><td>160</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>1</td><td>1,6</td><td>250</td><td>312,5</td><td>250</td></tr>\n  ```\n  \n  Row 3 (class 1): cells: 1 (accuracy class), 1 (Q1), 1,6 (Q2), 160 (Q3), 200 (Q4), 160 (ratio). ✓\n  Row 4 (class 1): cells: 1 (Q1), 1,6 (Q2), 250 (Q3), 312,5 (Q4), 250 (ratio). ✓\n\n- DN125, class 2, two rows (ratio 400):\n  - 0.625 | 1 | 250 | 312.5\n  - 1 | 1.6 | 400 | 500\n  Then class 1, two rows:\n  - 1.56 | 2.5 | 250 | 312.5, ratio 160\n  - 1.6 | 2.56 | 400 | 500, ratio 250\n\n- DN150, class 2, two rows (ratio 400):\n  - 1 | 1.6 | 400 | 500\n  - 1.575 | 2.52 | 630 | 787.5\n  Then class 1, two rows:\n  - 2.5 | 4 | 400 | 500, ratio 160\n  - 2.52 | 4.03 | 630 | 787.5, ratio 250\n\n- DN200, three rows:\n  - class 2: 1.575 | 2.52 | 630 | 787.5, ratio 400\n  - class 1: 3.94 | 6.3 | 630 | 787.5, ratio 160\n  - class 2: 2.5 | 4 | 1000 | 1250, ratio 400\n\nSo the models: each DN size has multiple variants (accuracy class + ratio combos). The variants are effectively distinguished by (DN, accuracy class, Q3, ratio).\n\nFor model_family models, I could use IDs like \"DN25\", \"DN40\", ... but multiple rows per DN exist. Per schema, model_level values are {model, value}. I could create model IDs like \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\", etc. But that might be over-engineering. Alternatively, use config_level with axis.\n\nHmm. The cleanest approach following the R137 example: models = DN sizes, and matrix_tables preserves the full table with all rows. Then model_level gives q1/q2/q3/q4 per model — but with multiple rows per DN, that's ambiguous.\n\nI think the best approach: \n- model_family.models with model_id per row-variant, e.g. \"DN25/2/400\" or more descriptive like \"DN25 (class 2, Q3=10)\". \n- Also populate matrix_tables with the full Table 2.\n\nActually, let me think about what's most useful and faithful. The schema example for R137 uses model_id = size (G1.6). Here, DN sizes alone don't uniquely identify variants. The distinguishing attributes within a DN are accuracy class and Q3/ratio.\n\nI'll create model IDs like \"DN25-Q3=10-cl2\", \"DN25-Q3=16-cl2\", \"DN40-Q3=25-cl2\", \"DN40-Q3=40-cl2\", \"DN50-Q3=40-cl2\", \"DN50-Q3=63-cl2\", \"DN65-Q3=63-cl2\", \"DN65-Q3=100-cl2\", \"DN65-Q3=100-cl1\", \"DN80-Q3=100-cl2\", \"DN80-Q3=160-cl2\", \"DN80-Q3=100-cl1\", \"DN80-Q3=160-cl1\", \"DN100-Q3=160-cl2\", \"DN100-Q3=250-cl2\", \"DN100-Q3=160-cl1\", \"DN100-Q3=250-cl1\", \"DN125-Q3=250-cl2\", \"DN125-Q3=400-cl2\", \"DN125-Q3=250-cl1\", \"DN125-Q3=400-cl1\", \"DN150-Q3=400-cl2\", \"DN150-Q3=630-cl2\", \"DN150-Q3=400-cl1\", \"DN150-Q3=630-cl1\", \"DN200-Q3=630-cl2\", \"DN200-Q3=630-cl1\", \"DN200-Q3=1000-cl2\".\n\nThat's 28 variants. That's a lot but faithful.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: model_id per DN size (9 models: DN25...DN200), and put the full variant matrix in matrix_tables, with model_level capturing meter_size only... Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The certificate's model designations are \"6500W + IMT656W\" — these are the type designations. The family is described by DN size and class. I think a hybrid is best:\n\n- model_family.family_name: null (no explicit family name; \"family of instruments\" is mentioned — could use \"family of instruments\"? The text says \"Table 2 gives an overview of the general characteristics of the family of instruments.\" No explicit family name. So null.)\n- models: list of 28 variants with model_id strings encoding DN/class/ratio, or 9 DN sizes.\n\nGiven the schema says model_level entries: attribute × model matrix with values per model, and the DN table has repeated DN sizes with different values, I'll go with variant-level model IDs. Let me use format \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10 m³/h\"... too verbose. Maybe \"DN25/Q3=10/cl2\".\n\nHmm, let me think about how the downstream consumer would use this. They'd want to query: for DN80, class 1, ratio 250: Q1=0.64 etc. Model IDs should be human-readable and unique.\n\nI'll use: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\", \"DN25-class2-Q3=16\", etc.\n\nActually, maybe cleaner: since ratio and class correlate: for each DN, class 2 variants have ratio 400; class 1 variants have ratio 160 or 250. Within a DN+class, variants differ by Q3. So \"DN65 class 1\" is unique (ratio 250), \"DN80 class 1\" has two variants (ratio 160, 250).\n\nFull list of variants (28):\n1. DN25, cl2, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, Q2=0.04, Q1=0.025, ratio 400\n2. DN25, cl2, Q3=16, Q4=20, Q2=0.064, Q1=0.04, ratio 400\n3. DN40, cl2, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, Q2=0.1, Q1=0.0625, ratio 400\n4. DN40, cl2, Q3=40, Q4=50, Q2=0.16, Q1=0.1, ratio 400\n5. DN50, cl2, Q3=40, Q4=50, Q2=0.16, Q1=0.1, ratio 400\n6. DN50, cl2, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, Q2=0.252, Q1=0.1575, ratio 400\n7. DN65, cl2, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, Q2=0.25, Q1=0.1575, ratio 400\n8. DN65, cl2, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q2=0.4, Q1=0.25, ratio 400\n9. DN65, cl1, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q2=0.64, Q1=0.4, ratio 250\n10. DN80, cl2, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q2=0.4, Q1=0.25, ratio 400\n11. DN80, cl2, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q2=0.64, Q1=0.4, ratio 400\n12. DN80, cl1, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q2=1, Q1=0.625, ratio 160\n13. DN80, cl1, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q2=1.02, Q1=0.64, ratio 250\n14. DN100, cl2, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q2=0.64, Q1=0.4, ratio 400\n15. DN100, cl2, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q2=1, Q1=0.625, ratio 400\n16. DN100, cl1, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q2=1.6, Q1=1, ratio 160\n17. DN100, cl1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q2=1.6, Q1=1, ratio 250\n18. DN125, cl2, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q2=1, Q1=0.625, ratio 400\n19. DN125, cl2, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q2=1.6, Q1=1, ratio 400\n20. DN125, cl1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q2=2.5, Q1=1.56, ratio 160\n21. DN125, cl1, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q2=2.56, Q1=1.6, ratio 250\n22. DN150, cl2, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q2=1.6, Q1=1, ratio 400\n23. DN150, cl2, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q2=2.52, Q1=1.575, ratio 400\n24. DN150, cl1, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q2=4, Q1=2.5, ratio 160\n25. DN150, cl1, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q2=4.03, Q1=2.52, ratio 250\n26. DN200, cl2, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q2=2.52, Q1=1.575, ratio 400\n27. DN200, cl1, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q2=6.3, Q1=3.94, ratio 160\n28. DN200, cl2, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, Q2=4, Q1=2.5, ratio 400\n\nModel ID format: I'll use \"DN25 Q3=10 cl 2\" style. Or maybe simpler \"DN25-10-2\". Hmm. Let me use something readable: \"DN25 Q3=10 class 2\". That's unambiguous.\n\nThen model_level attributes:\n- meter_size (DN) — value \"DN25\" string? Could include as attribute with string values. Hmm, meter_size is part of model id; maybe skip separate attribute. Actually including it helps querying. I'll include \"nominal_size\" with values like \"DN25\".\n- accuracy_class: 1 or 2\n- q1 (minimum flow rate), q2 (transitional), q3 (permanent), q4 (overload) — unit m³/h\n- ratio_q3_q1: 400/250/160\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: MAP depends on DN: 1.6 MPa for DN200 and smaller; 1.0 MPa for DN250 and larger. All models here are ≤ DN200, so 1.6 MPa applies to all. That could be type_level with a note, or model_level... Since all models are DN≤200, it's effectively constant. But the rule states DN200 and smaller → 1.6 MPa. I could put it in type_level as structured value with footnote-like explanation, or config_level. I'll put it in type_level: value 1.6 MPa with... hmm, but the condition matters. Maybe record both: map_dn200_and_smaller: 1.6 MPa, map_dn250_and_larger: 1.0 MPa as type_level? That's awkward. Alternatively config_level with axis \"meter_size\": condition \"DN ≤ 200\" → 1.6 MPa; \"DN ≥ 250\" → 1.0 MPa. That fits config_level nicely: attribute \"maximum_admissible_pressure\", axis \"meter_size\", values: [{condition: \"DN200 and smaller\", value 1.6 MPa}, {condition: \"DN250 and larger\", value 1.0}]. I'll do that.\n\n**Type-level characteristics (Table 1):**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1; 2\" — this is covered per model; as type_level could be list... Rule says lists of values → model matrix. Accuracy class varies by model, so it's in model_level. But type-level \"accuracy classes covered: 1, 2\" also goes into recommendation.accuracy_classes. I'll put accuracy_class in model_level per variant and in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [1, 2].\n- environmental_class: \"C (fixes meters, installed outdoors)\" → value \"C\", description? I'll record value \"C\" with maybe note. Actually keep as string \"C\" and maybe a separate attribute environmental_class_description: \"fixed meters, installed outdoors\". Let me record environmental_class: {value: \"C\"} and add the parenthetical to description or as footnote... I'll make value \"C\" and add attribute environmental_class_application: \"fixed meters, installed outdoors\". Hmm, maybe simpler: value \"C\" only, and the parenthetical in certified_type.description? No — description is about the type. I'll include environmental_class: {value: \"C\", unit: null} and skip the parenthetical? The instructions say don't fabricate but extracting faithfully is good. I'll record environmental_class value \"C\" and environmental_class_note \"fixed meters, installed outdoors\". Hmm, adding extra attributes is fine as long as they're grounded. Actually simpler: keep the full string as value: \"C (fixed meters, installed outdoors)\". Structured value allows strings. I'll do value \"C\" plus note attribute. Let me just do the string with the full content — it's faithful. But it includes \"fixes\" typo from OCR (\"fixes meters\" probably \"fixed meters\"). I'll keep as-is? Rule: don't fabricate; OCR text says \"fixes meters\". I'll write \"C\" as value and include note. I'll do: environmental_class: {value: \"C\"} and environmental_class_description: {value: \"fixed meters, installed outdoors\"}. Hmm, minor normalization \"fixes\" → \"fixed\" is reasonable. Actually I'll keep exactly \"fixes meters, installed outdoors\"? That looks wrong. I'll normalize to \"fixed meters, installed outdoors\" — acceptable.\n\nLet me not overthink; value \"C\" for environmental_class.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: -25 °C / +55 °C → {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: T50 (+0.1 °C / +50 °C) → class T50; range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. Record water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, °C.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: config_level as discussed.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0D upstream and 0D downstream)\" → value \"U0/D0\"? Record as string \"U0 and D0\". Maybe two attributes: upstream \"U0\", downstream \"D0\". I'll record flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"}.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → boolean-ish; record as string \"intended to measure reverse flow\" or value true? I'll record reverse_flow: {value: true}? Hmm, string is safer: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". I'll use value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"... Actually faithful: keep the string. I'll use \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — but canonical labels say avoid trailing punctuation. Value strings can contain sentences though. I'll simplify: reverse_flow_supported: {value: true}. Hmm risk of interpretation. I'll record attribute reverse_flow with value \"measures reverse flow\" — eh. Let me just record value true with attribute reverse_flow_measurement. Hmm.\n\nI'll go with: \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": true}. It's a fair encoding of \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\". Or keep string. I'll keep the string to be safe: value: \"Sensor intended to measure reverse flow\". Meh. Decision: use true for boolean semantics. Actually, the schema example in R137 characteristics uses value true/false never shown... Values can be strings per spec. I'll use string \"intended to measure reverse flow\" — no. FINAL: {\"value\": true} for reverse_flow. Hmm, but \"intended to\" — yes it measures reverse flow. OK true.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: Δp 63 (0.63 bar) → class \"Δp63\", value 0.63 bar. Record pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"}? Canonical labels should not include LaTeX; Δp is fine Unicode. I'll record pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp63\"} and max_pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit: \"bar\"}. \n\n- power supply: multiple options:\n  - Battery 3.6 V\n  - External battery pack with output 3.6 V\n  - FlexPower 10…30 VDC or 110…230 VAC / 50-60 Hz\n  \n  Record as components? No — these are power supply options. Record:\n  - power_supply_battery: {value: 3.6, unit \"V\"}\n  - power_supply_external_battery_pack_output: {value: 3.6, \"V\"}\n  - power_supply_flexpower_voltage_dc: {min:10, max:30, \"V\"}\n  - power_supply_flexpower_voltage_ac: {min:110, max:230, \"V\"}\n  - power_supply_flexpower_frequency: {min:50, max:60, \"Hz\"}\n\n- software_identification / CRC checksum: pairs:\n  - 5.0.1_ / 4CB5\n  - 5.0.2_ / 71D5\n  - 5.0.3_ / CFF7\n  - 5.0.5_ / DCAB\n  Record as... maybe matrix? Or a list of objects. I could record software_versions as a matrix table or as individual attributes. Options: type_level software_identification with value listing all? Rule: pure lists... These are version/CRC pairs. I'll create config_level? Axis \"software version\": condition \"5.0.1_\" → CRC \"4CB5\", etc. That fits config_level: attribute \"crc_checksum\", axis \"software_version\", values: [{condition: \"5.0.1_\", value: \"4CB5\"}, ...]. \n\n  Alternatively matrix_tables entry. config_level seems apt. Or I could add attributes software_version_1..4 — ugly. I'll use config_level.\n\n  Hmm, but config_level value shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Value \"4CB5\" string. Good.\n\n**Documentation folder:** \"The construction of the measuring instrument is recorded 